ents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n87,FACTORIAL EXPERIMENT,\"### Comprehensive Summary of Factorial Experiment\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nA Factorial Experiment is a type of experimental design that investigates the effects of two or more factors on a response variable by varying these factors simultaneously. The primary purpose of a factorial experiment is to understand not only the individual effects of each factor but also the interactions between them, which can provide insights into complex systems and processes.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nThe general model for a factorial experiment can be expressed as:  \n\\[ Y_{ijk} = \\mu + \\alpha_i + \\beta_j + (\\alpha\\beta)_{ij} + \\epsilon_{ijk} \\]  \nwhere \\( Y_{ijk} \\) is the response variable, \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean, \\( \\alpha_i \\) represents the effect of the \\( i \\)-th level of factor A, \\( \\beta_j \\) represents the effect of the \\( j \\)-th level of factor B, \\( (\\alpha\\beta)_{ij} \\) represents the interaction effect between factors A and B, and \\( \\epsilon_{ijk} \\) is the random error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nFactorial experiments can be structured in various layouts, including full factorial designs, where all possible combinations of factor levels are tested, and fractional factorial designs, which test only a subset of combinations to reduce the number of experimental runs. The layout typically includes factors, levels, and the response variable, organized in a systematic manner to facilitate analysis.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of factorial experiments relies on several key assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations: The experimental units must be independent.\n2. Normality: The residuals should be normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variance: The variance among groups should be approximately equal (homoscedasticity).\n4. Linearity: The relationship between factors and the response should be linear.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nFactorial experiments are particularly useful when researchers want to explore the effects of multiple factors and their interactions on a response variable. They are applicable in various fields, including agriculture, manufacturing, and social sciences. Selection criteria include the number of factors of interest, the need to understand interactions, and resource availability for conducting multiple experimental runs.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of factorial experiments typically involves Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to determine the significance of main effects and interactions. The procedure includes:\n1. Formulating hypotheses for each factor and interaction.\n2. Conducting ANOVA to partition the total variance into components attributable to each factor and their interactions.\n3. Performing post-hoc tests if necessary to identify specific differences between factor levels.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nFactorial experiments offer several advantages:\n- They allow for the simultaneous examination of multiple factors and their interactions, providing a comprehensive understanding of the system.\n- They are efficient in terms of resource utilization, as they can yield more information from fewer experimental runs compared to one-factor-at-a-time approaches.\n- They facilitate the identification of optimal conditions for the response variable.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nDespite their strengths, factorial experiments have limitations:\n- The complexity increases with the number of factors and levels, making analysis and interpretation more challenging.\n- Full factorial designs can become impractical with a large number of factors due to the exponential increase in the number of experimental runs required.\n- Interaction effects can complicate the analysis and may require larger sample sizes to detect.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated designs include fractional factorial designs, which reduce the number of runs by testing only a fraction of the full factorial combinations. Other alternatives include response surface methodology (RSM) for optimizing processes and Taguchi methods for robust design.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing factorial experiments, researchers should consider the following:\n- The selection of appropriate factors and levels based on prior knowledge or exploratory studies.\n- Randomization of experimental runs to mitigate bias.\n- Replication of runs to enhance the reliability of results.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nFactorial experiments can be analyzed using various statistical software packages, including R, SAS, Minitab, and SPSS, which provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA and generating interaction plots.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nFactorial experiments are widely used in various applications, such as:\n- Evaluating the effects of different fertilizers and irrigation levels on crop yield.\n- Studying the impact of temperature and pressure on the quality of manufactured products, as exemplified by Chrysler Motors in their evaluations.\n- Investigating the filling variability of dry soup mix packages by analyzing factors affecting the packaging process.\n\nIn summary, factorial experiments are a powerful tool in the design of experiments, enabling researchers to explore complex interactions among multiple factors efficiently and effectively.\",38\r\n320,COMPLETE BLOCK DESIGN,\"An experimental design method where every block receives all treatments, allowing for comprehensive comparison of treatment effects.\",2\r\n520,CONFOUNDING ARRANGEMENTS,Confounding arrangements refer to the method of organizing treatment combinations in statistical experiments to control for confounding factors.,2\r\n312,RBD,\"**Randomized Block Design (RBD) Summary**\n\nRandomized Block Design (RBD) is a statistical design employed in experimental research to control for variability among experimental units. The primary purpose of RBD is to reduce the impact of confounding variables by grouping similar experimental units into blocks, thereby allowing for a more accurate assessment of the treatment effects.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation and Model Structure:**\nIn RBD, the experimental units are divided into blocks, each containing a set of treatments. The model can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y_{ij} = \\mu + \\tau_j + \\beta_i + \\epsilon_{ij} \\]\nwhere:\n- \\( Y_{ij} \\) is the response variable for the \\( j \\)-th treatment in the \\( i \\)-th block,\n- \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean,\n- \\( \\tau_j \\) is the effect of the \\( j \\)-th treatment,\n- \\( \\beta_i \\) is the effect of the \\( i \\)-th block,\n- \\( \\epsilon_{ij} \\) is the random error associated with the \\( j \\)-th treatment in the \\( i \\)-th block.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nThe structure of RBD involves the following steps:\n1. Identify the experimental units and the blocking factor(s).\n2. Group the experimental units into blocks based on the blocking factor(s).\n3. Randomly assign treatments within each block.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of RBD relies on several key assumptions:\n- The experimental units within each block are homogeneous.\n- The treatment effects are additive and independent of the blocks.\n- The errors are normally distributed with constant variance.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nRBD is particularly useful when there are known sources of variability that can be controlled through blocking. It is applicable in situations where:\n- The experimental units can be grouped based on a blocking factor.\n- There is a need to improve the precision of treatment comparisons by accounting for variability.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of RBD typically involves:\n1. Conducting an Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to test for significant treatment effects.\n2. Estimating the treatment means and comparing them using post-hoc tests if necessary.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- RBD effectively reduces variability, leading to increased statistical power.\n- It allows for a clearer interpretation of treatment effects by controlling for confounding factors.\n- The design is flexible and can be adapted to various experimental contexts.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- RBD requires knowledge of the blocking factor and may not be effective if the blocks are not well-defined.\n- The design can become complex with multiple blocking factors or treatments, complicating analysis and interpretation.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRBD is related to other experimental designs such as Completely Randomized Design (CRD) and Factorial Design. Alternatives may include Latin Square Design when there are two blocking factors or Split-Plot Design for hierarchical structures.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing RBD, researchers should ensure that the blocking factor is relevant and that the randomization process is strictly followed to maintain the integrity of the design. Adequate sample sizes within blocks are also crucial for reliable results.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nRBD can be analyzed using various statistical software packages, including R, SAS, and SPSS, which provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA and generating relevant statistical outputs.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nRBD is commonly used in agricultural experiments, clinical trials, and industrial quality control studies, where it is essential to account for variability among experimental units to accurately assess treatment effects.\",3\r\n100,FRACTIONAL REPLICATIONS,\"**Comprehensive Summary of Fractional Replications**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nFractional replications are a type of experimental design used in the field of Design of Experiments (DOE) that enables researchers to investigate a fraction of the full factorial experiment. This approach is particularly useful when the full factorial design would require an impractically large number of experimental runs, making it difficult or impossible to conduct. By studying a subset of treatment combinations, fractional replications allow for efficient exploration of the effects of multiple factors on a response variable while conserving resources.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nIn a fractional replication design, the full factorial experiment is represented as \\(2^k\\), where \\(k\\) is the number of factors. A fractional design typically involves selecting a fraction of these runs, denoted as \\(2^{k-p}\\), where \\(p\\) indicates the degree of fractionation. The selection of runs is often based on a generator, which defines the specific combinations of factors to be included in the experiment.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nThe layout of a fractional replication design is structured around the selected treatment combinations. For example, in a \\(2^{k-p}\\) design, the treatments are organized in a way that allows for the estimation of main effects and possibly some interactions, depending on the chosen fraction. The design can be represented in a tabular format, where each row corresponds to a treatment combination and each column represents a factor.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of fractional replications relies on several key assumptions:\n1. The effects of the factors are additive, meaning that interactions are either negligible or can be estimated.\n2. The experimental errors are normally distributed and have constant variance (homoscedasticity).\n3. The observations are independent of one another.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nFractional replications are particularly applicable when:\n- The number of factors is large, making a full factorial design impractical.\n- Resources (time, budget, materials) are limited.\n- The researcher is primarily interested in estimating main effects and possibly low-order interactions.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of fractional replication designs typically involves the use of Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to assess the significance of the factors and their interactions. The analysis may also include the use of regression techniques to model the relationship between factors and the response variable.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nThe advantages of fractional replications include:\n- Significant reduction in the number of experimental runs required, leading to cost and time savings.\n- The ability to explore a wide range of factors and their effects without the need for exhaustive experimentation.\n- Flexibility in design, allowing researchers to focus on the most relevant factors.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nHowever, fractional replications also have limitations:\n- The potential for confounding, where the effects of certain factors may be indistinguishable from others, particularly if higher-order interactions are not negligible.\n- The need for careful selection of the fraction to ensure that important effects are not missed.\n- The complexity of analysis increases as the number of factors and interactions increases.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated designs include full factorial designs, Plackett-Burman designs, and response surface methodologies. Each of these alternatives has its own strengths and weaknesses, depending on the specific research objectives and constraints.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing fractional replications, researchers should consider the following:\n- The choice of fraction should be guided by the specific goals of the study and the expected interactions.\n- Adequate randomization and replication should be employed to ensure the reliability of results.\n- The design should be pilot-tested if possible to identify any unforeseen issues.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nMany statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and Minitab, provide tools for designing and analyzing fractional replication experiments. These tools often include built-in functions for generating designs, conducting ANOVA, and visualizing results.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nFractional replications are commonly used in industrial experiments, agricultural studies, and product development, where multiple factors influence outcomes, and resource constraints necessitate a more efficient experimental approach. Examples include optimizing manufacturing processes, evaluating agricultural treatments, and testing new product formulations.\",2\r\n2036,DRY SOUP MIX PACKAGES,The dry soup mix packages are the subject of a factorial experiment to study filling variability.,2\r\n102,ESTIMATES,\"### Comprehensive Summary of Estimates in Design of Experiments\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nEstimates are calculated values that approximate a population parameter based on sample data. In the context of factorial experiments, estimates specifically refer to the calculated effects of factors and their interactions derived from experimental data. These estimates serve to provide insights into the relationships between independent variables (factors) and the dependent variable (response), facilitating informed decision-making in experimental design and analysis.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nIn factorial experiments, estimates can be represented mathematically through the model structure:  \n\\[ Y = \\mu + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\beta_i X_i + \\sum_{j=1}^{k} \\sum_{i<j} \\beta_{ij} X_i X_j + \\epsilon \\]  \nwhere \\( Y \\) is the response variable, \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean, \\( \\beta_i \\) are the main effects of factors \\( X_i \\), \\( \\beta_{ij} \\) are the interaction effects, and \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nEstimates are derived from the layout of factorial designs, which can be either full factorial or fractional factorial. In a full factorial design, all possible combinations of factor levels are tested, allowing for comprehensive estimation of main effects and interactions. In contrast, fractional factorial designs involve a subset of combinations, which can be more efficient in terms of resources while still providing useful estimates.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of estimates relies on several key assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations: The experimental units must be independent.\n2. Normality: The residuals (errors) should be normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variance: The variance among groups should be approximately equal (homoscedasticity).\n4. Linearity: The relationship between factors and the response should be linear.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nEstimates are applicable in various experimental contexts, particularly when researchers aim to understand the effects of multiple factors on a response variable. They are particularly useful in factorial designs where interactions between factors are of interest. Selection criteria include the number of factors, the nature of the response variable, and the resources available for experimentation.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of estimates typically involves the use of Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to determine the significance of the estimated effects. The procedure includes:\n1. Formulating the null and alternative hypotheses.\n2. Calculating the ANOVA table, which includes sums of squares, degrees of freedom, mean squares, and F-statistics.\n3. Conducting post-hoc tests if necessary to explore significant differences among factor levels.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nEstimates provide a quantitative basis for understanding the effects of factors and their interactions. They allow for the identification of significant factors that influence the response variable, enabling optimization of processes and products. Additionally, estimates derived from factorial designs can lead to more efficient experimental designs by revealing interactions that may not be apparent in simpler designs.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nThe primary limitations of estimates include the reliance on the assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variance, which, if violated, can lead to inaccurate conclusions. Furthermore, in fractional factorial designs, important interactions may be confounded, leading to potential misinterpretation of results. Additionally, estimates can be sensitive to outliers, which can skew results.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nEstimates are closely related to various experimental designs, including response surface methodology (RSM) and Taguchi methods, which also focus on optimizing responses based on factor levels. Alternatives to factorial designs include completely randomized designs and block designs, which may be more suitable in certain experimental contexts.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing estimates in practice, researchers should ensure proper randomization and replication to enhance the reliability of the estimates. It is also crucial to conduct preliminary analyses to check the assumptions of the model before proceeding with the estimation and interpretation of results.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nEstimates can be computed using various statistical software packages, including R, SAS, and Minitab, which provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA and generating estimates of effects and interactions.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nEstimates are commonly used in industrial experiments to optimize manufacturing processes, in agricultural studies to evaluate the effects of fertilizers on crop yield, and in clinical trials to assess the efficacy of treatments across different patient demographics. These applications highlight the versatility and importance of estimates in experimental research.\",1\r\n2037,MIXING PORTS,Mixing ports are the points through which vegetable oil is added during the filling process.,1\r\n2038,MIXING TIME,\"Mixing time is a variable in the filling process, measured in seconds.\",1\r\n2039,BATCH WEIGHT,\"Batch weight refers to the total weight of the soup mix being processed, measured in pounds.\",1\r\n2040,PACKAGE WEIGHT,\"Package weight is the response variable measured during the experiment, indicating the standard deviation of the weight of the packages.\",1\r\n2047,FILLING VARIABILITY,,1\r\n2041,SIGN CHANGES,\"### Comprehensive Summary of Sign Changes in Experimental Design\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nSign changes in experimental design refer to the adjustments made to the signs of factors in the design matrix, which can significantly influence the analysis of variance (ANOVA) and the interpretation of results. The primary purpose of implementing sign changes is to explore the effects of different factors on the response variable, particularly in factorial designs where interactions between factors are of interest.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nIn the context of factorial designs, the design matrix is typically represented as a matrix of coded levels for each factor. A sign change involves altering the signs of certain factors, which can be mathematically represented as follows:\n\n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_1 + \\beta_2 X_2 + \\ldots + \\beta_k X_k + \\epsilon \\]\n\nWhere \\( Y \\) is the response variable, \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept, \\( \\beta_i \\) are the coefficients for each factor \\( X_i \\), and \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the error term. A sign change for a factor \\( X_j \\) would modify its coefficient \\( \\beta_j \\) to \\( -\\beta_j \\).\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nIn factorial designs, sign changes can be applied to any of the factors involved. The design layout remains the same, but the interpretation of the effects may change based on the sign modifications. For example, in a 2^k factorial design, each factor can take on two levels, and sign changes can be applied to any combination of these factors.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of sign changes relies on several assumptions:\n1. Linearity: The relationship between factors and the response variable is linear.\n2. Independence: Observations are independent of one another.\n3. Homoscedasticity: The variance of the residuals is constant across all levels of the factors.\n4. Normality: The residuals should be normally distributed.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nSign changes are particularly applicable in situations where interactions between factors are suspected or when exploring the robustness of the model. They are useful in identifying potential confounding effects and in refining the model to better fit the observed data.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis procedure following sign changes involves re-evaluating the model using ANOVA to assess the significance of the factors and their interactions. This includes calculating the sum of squares for each factor, determining the F-statistic, and conducting post-hoc tests if necessary.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nThe advantages of implementing sign changes include:\n- Enhanced understanding of factor interactions.\n- Improved model fit by identifying significant factors.\n- Flexibility in exploring different configurations of the design.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nHowever, there are limitations to consider:\n- Sign changes can complicate the interpretation of results, especially in the presence of multiple interactions.\n- They may lead to overfitting if not carefully managed.\n- The potential for increased complexity in the analysis and reporting of results.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated methods include response surface methodology (RSM) and other factorial designs that allow for the exploration of interactions. Alternatives may involve using different coding schemes or transformations to assess the effects of factors without changing their signs.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing sign changes, researchers should carefully document the rationale for changes and ensure that the implications for interpretation are clear. It is also important to consider the potential impact on the overall design and analysis strategy.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nMany statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and Minitab, provide functionalities for conducting ANOVA and managing design matrices, including the ability to apply sign changes. Users should familiarize themselves with the specific commands and procedures relevant to their chosen software.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nSign changes are commonly applied in industrial experiments, agricultural studies, and any field where factorial designs are utilized to optimize processes or products. For instance, in a study examining the effects of temperature and pressure on yield, sign changes may help clarify the nature of interactions between these factors.\",1\r\n2042,DESIRABLE LAYER THICKNESS,\"Desirable layer thickness is the target range for the thickness of the soup mix layer, specified as between 140 and 160.\",1\r\n2043,26² DESIGN,The 26² design is a specific experimental design used in the study to analyze the effects of various factors on filling variability.,1\r\n2044,27 IV (2)³ DESIGN,The 27 IV (2)³ design is another experimental design referenced in the context of analyzing filling variability.,1\r\n89,INTERACTION EFFECT,\"**Interaction Effect**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**: An interaction effect occurs when the effect of one independent variable (factor) on the response variable is influenced by the level of another independent variable. This concept is crucial in the analysis of experimental designs, as it helps to understand how different factors work together to affect the outcome, rather than in isolation.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation**: In a typical two-factor analysis, the interaction effect can be represented in a linear model as follows:\n\n\\[ Y_{ijk} = \\mu + \\alpha_i + \\beta_j + (\\alpha\\beta)_{ij} + \\epsilon_{ijk} \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( Y_{ijk} \\) is the response variable,\n- \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean,\n- \\( \\alpha_i \\) is the effect of the i-th level of factor A,\n- \\( \\beta_j \\) is the effect of the j-th level of factor B,\n- \\( (\\alpha\\beta)_{ij} \\) is the interaction effect between factors A and B,\n- \\( \\epsilon_{ijk} \\) is the random error term.\n\n**Design Layout**: Interaction effects are typically assessed in factorial designs, where multiple factors are varied simultaneously. A full factorial design allows for the estimation of all main effects and interaction effects, while a fractional factorial design may be used when resources are limited.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**: The analysis of interaction effects assumes:\n1. Independence of observations.\n2. Normality of the residuals.\n3. Homogeneity of variances (homoscedasticity).\n4. Linearity of the relationship between factors and the response variable.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**: Interaction effects should be considered when:\n- There is a theoretical basis to expect that the effect of one factor may depend on another.\n- The experimental design includes multiple factors that are likely to interact.\n- The goal is to optimize a response variable based on multiple influencing factors.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**: To analyze interaction effects, one typically conducts an Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) that includes interaction terms. The significance of interaction effects can be tested using F-tests, and if significant, further investigation into the nature of the interaction (e.g., simple effects analysis) may be warranted.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**: \n- Understanding interaction effects provides deeper insights into the relationships between factors.\n- It can lead to more accurate models and predictions by capturing the complexity of real-world phenomena.\n- Identifying significant interactions can inform better decision-making in experimental settings.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**: \n- Interaction effects can complicate the interpretation of results, especially in higher-order interactions.\n- They require larger sample sizes to estimate accurately, particularly in factorial designs with many factors.\n- Overfitting can occur if too many interaction terms are included in the model without sufficient data.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**: Interaction effects are commonly analyzed in factorial designs, but can also be explored in response surface methodology (RSM) and mixed models. Alternatives include using regression analysis to model interactions in observational data.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**: When designing experiments to assess interaction effects, researchers should ensure that the levels of each factor are adequately represented and that the design is balanced to avoid confounding. It is also important to consider the potential for higher-order interactions, which may require more complex designs.\n\n**Software Implementation**: Many statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and SPSS, provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA and estimating interaction effects. Users can specify interaction terms in their models to facilitate analysis.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**: Interaction effects are frequently studied in various fields, including agriculture (e.g., the combined impact of temperature and pressure on crop yield), manufacturing (e.g., the interaction between machine settings and material types), and social sciences (e.g., the interaction between demographic factors and behavioral outcomes). Understanding these interactions can lead to improved processes and outcomes in these domains.\",4\r\n74,BLOCK,\"**Entity: BLOCK**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nA block in experimental design is defined as a group of experimental units that share similar characteristics, which are expected to influence the response to the treatments applied. The primary purpose of blocking is to reduce variability in the experimental results, thereby increasing the precision of the estimates of treatment effects. By grouping similar experimental units, researchers can control for the effects of nuisance variables that may confound the results.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nIn a typical block design, the model can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y_{ij} = \\mu + \\tau_i + \\beta_j + \\epsilon_{ij} \\]\nwhere:\n- \\( Y_{ij} \\) is the response variable for the \\( j \\)-th unit in the \\( i \\)-th block,\n- \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean,\n- \\( \\tau_i \\) is the effect of the \\( i \\)-th treatment,\n- \\( \\beta_j \\) is the effect of the \\( j \\)-th block,\n- \\( \\epsilon_{ij} \\) is the random error associated with the \\( j \\)-th unit in the \\( i \\)-th block.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nBlocks are structured in various designs, such as Randomized Block Design (RBD) and Balanced Incomplete Block Design (BIBD). In RBD, treatments are randomly assigned within each block, while in BIBD, not all treatments are applied to every block, allowing for a more flexible design when the number of treatments exceeds the number of experimental units.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of block designs relies on several assumptions:\n1. The experimental units within each block are homogeneous.\n2. The treatment effects are additive and independent of the block effects.\n3. The errors are normally distributed and have constant variance (homoscedasticity).\n4. The randomization of treatment assignments within blocks is properly executed.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nBlocking is particularly useful when there are known sources of variability that can be controlled. It is applicable in situations where:\n- There are identifiable factors that can be grouped (e.g., time, location, or batch).\n- The number of experimental units is sufficient to form blocks without compromising the number of treatments.\n- The researcher aims to improve the precision of treatment comparisons.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of block designs typically involves Analysis of Variance (ANOVA), where the total variability is partitioned into components attributable to treatments, blocks, and error. The F-test is used to determine if there are significant differences among treatment means after accounting for block effects.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Reduces variability, leading to more precise estimates of treatment effects.\n- Increases the power of statistical tests by controlling for nuisance factors.\n- Facilitates the analysis of experiments with limited resources by maximizing the use of available experimental units.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- Requires prior knowledge of the blocking factor, which may not always be available.\n- The effectiveness of blocking diminishes if the blocks are not well-defined or if there is significant interaction between treatments and blocks.\n- Can complicate the design and analysis if the number of treatments and blocks is not balanced.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated designs include:\n- Randomized Complete Block Design (RCBD)\n- Latin Square Design\n- Factorial Designs with blocking\nAlternatives to blocking include stratified sampling and covariate adjustment in analysis.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing block designs, researchers should ensure that:\n- Blocks are defined based on relevant characteristics that influence the response.\n- Randomization is properly executed within blocks to avoid bias.\n- Sufficient sample sizes are allocated to each block to maintain statistical power.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nMany statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and SPSS, provide built-in functions for designing and analyzing block designs, including ANOVA procedures tailored for block designs.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nBlocking is commonly used in agricultural experiments (e.g., field trials), clinical trials, and industrial experiments where environmental or operational conditions can vary. For instance, in agricultural studies, blocks may represent different plots of land with varying soil types, while in clinical trials, blocks may represent different patient demographics.\",27\r\n90,PLOTS,\"**Comprehensive Summary of PLOTS in Experimental Design**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nPLOTS are defined as the experimental units or areas within a design framework where specific treatments are applied and subsequently measured. They serve as the fundamental building blocks of experimental designs, allowing researchers to assess the effects of various treatments on the response variables of interest.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nIn the context of experimental designs, particularly factorial experiments and partially balanced incomplete block designs (PBIBD), the treatment effects can be modeled using linear models. For instance, in a factorial experiment, the model can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y_{ijk} = \\mu + \\alpha_i + \\beta_j + (\\alpha\\beta)_{ij} + \\epsilon_{ijk} \\]\nwhere \\( Y_{ijk} \\) is the response variable, \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean, \\( \\alpha_i \\) represents the effect of the \\( i \\)-th treatment, \\( \\beta_j \\) represents the effect of the \\( j \\)-th factor, and \\( \\epsilon_{ijk} \\) is the random error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nIn factorial experiments, plots are arranged in a systematic manner to ensure that all combinations of treatments are represented. In PBIBD, plots are grouped into blocks, with each block containing a subset of treatments, allowing for efficient use of resources while controlling for variability.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of results obtained from plots relies on several assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations: The responses from different plots should be independent.\n2. Normality: The response variable should be approximately normally distributed within each treatment group.\n3. Homogeneity of variance: The variance of the response variable should be constant across different treatment groups.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nPLOTS are applicable in various experimental designs, including factorial designs and PBIBD, particularly when researchers aim to evaluate the effects of multiple treatments or factors. The selection of plots is crucial when the experimental units are heterogeneous, and blocking is necessary to control for variability.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of data collected from plots typically involves Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to determine if there are statistically significant differences among treatment means. The ANOVA framework allows for the partitioning of total variability into components attributable to treatments and error.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- PLOTS facilitate the systematic investigation of treatment effects, allowing for the evaluation of interactions between factors.\n- They enable efficient resource allocation and control of variability through blocking.\n- The use of plots in factorial designs enhances the ability to draw conclusions about multiple factors simultaneously.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- The requirement for independence among plots can be challenging to meet in practice, especially in field experiments.\n- The assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variance may not always hold, potentially leading to invalid conclusions.\n- In PBIBD, the incomplete nature of treatment assignment can complicate the analysis and interpretation of results.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nPLOTS are closely related to other experimental designs such as completely randomized designs (CRD) and randomized block designs (RBD). Alternatives to factorial designs include response surface methodology (RSM) for exploring optimal conditions.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing plots, researchers should consider the spatial arrangement of experimental units to minimize variability and ensure proper randomization. Careful planning of treatment assignments and replication is essential for robust experimental outcomes.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nStatistical software packages such as R, SAS, and SPSS provide tools for designing experiments involving plots and conducting ANOVA. These tools facilitate the analysis of complex designs and the interpretation of results.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nPLOTS are commonly used in agricultural experiments to evaluate the effects of different fertilizers or treatments on crop yield. They are also utilized in industrial settings for quality control experiments, where the impact of various factors on product performance is assessed.\",2\r\n521,INDEPENDENT SET,An independent set in statistical terms is a collection of effects or contrasts that do not influence each other in a confounding arrangement.,2\r\n77,FACTOR AB,Factor AB represents the interaction between Factor A and Factor B in the factorial experiment.,1\r\n78,FACTOR AC,Factor AC represents the interaction between Factor A and Factor C in the factorial experiment.,1\r\n79,FACTOR BC,Factor BC represents the interaction between Factor B and Factor C in the factorial experiment.,1\r\n80,FACTOR ABC,\"Factor ABC represents the interaction among Factors A, B, and C in the factorial experiment.\",1\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n87,FACTORIAL EXPERIMENT,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,\"**Comprehensive Summary of Factorial Experiment and Analysis of Variance**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nA factorial experiment is a type of experimental design that investigates the effects of two or more factors simultaneously, allowing researchers to evaluate not only the individual effects of each factor but also their interactions. The primary purpose of factorial experiments is to understand how different factors influence a response variable, providing insights that can lead to optimized processes or products.\n\nAnalysis of Variance (ANOVA) is a statistical method used to analyze the differences among group means in a sample. It is particularly useful in the context of factorial experiments, where it assesses the effects of different factors and their interactions on the response variable. ANOVA helps determine whether the observed variations in the data are statistically significant.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nIn a factorial experiment, the model can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y_{ijk} = \\mu + \\alpha_i + \\beta_j + (\\alpha\\beta)_{ij} + \\epsilon_{ijk} \\]\nwhere:\n- \\( Y_{ijk} \\) is the response variable,\n- \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean,\n- \\( \\alpha_i \\) is the effect of the \\( i \\)-th level of factor A,\n- \\( \\beta_j \\) is the effect of the \\( j \\)-th level of factor B,\n- \\( (\\alpha\\beta)_{ij} \\) is the interaction effect between factors A and B,\n- \\( \\epsilon_{ijk} \\) is the random error associated with the \\( k \\)-th observation.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nFactorial experiments are structured in a way that all possible combinations of factor levels are tested. For example, in a 2x2 factorial design, there are two levels for each of two factors, resulting in four treatment combinations. The layout can be represented in a matrix format, where rows correspond to different treatment combinations and columns represent the factors.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of ANOVA in factorial experiments relies on several assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations.\n2. Normality of the response variable within each group.\n3. Homogeneity of variances across groups (homoscedasticity).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nFactorial experiments are applicable when researchers want to study the effects of multiple factors and their interactions on a response variable. They are particularly useful when:\n- The factors are categorical.\n- The number of levels for each factor is manageable.\n- Interaction effects are of interest.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of factorial experiments using ANOVA involves the following steps:\n1. Formulate the null and alternative hypotheses for each factor and interaction.\n2. Calculate the sum of squares for each source of variation (factors, interactions, and error).\n3. Compute the mean squares by dividing the sum of squares by their respective degrees of freedom.\n4. Calculate the F-statistic for each factor and interaction.\n5. Compare the F-statistic to the critical value from the F-distribution to determine significance.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Factorial experiments allow for the examination of interactions between factors, providing a more comprehensive understanding of the system being studied.\n- They are efficient, as multiple factors can be studied simultaneously without requiring a separate experiment for each factor.\n- ANOVA provides a clear framework for testing hypotheses about factor effects.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- The complexity of analysis increases with the number of factors and levels, which can lead to difficulties in interpretation.\n- If assumptions of ANOVA are violated, the results may be misleading.\n- Factorial designs can become impractical with a large number of factors due to the exponential increase in treatment combinations.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated designs include fractional factorial designs, which allow for the study of a subset of factor combinations, and response surface methodology, which focuses on optimizing responses. Alternatives to ANOVA include non-parametric methods when assumptions are not met.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing factorial experiments, researchers should consider the feasibility of testing all combinations of factors, the availability of resources, and the potential for confounding variables. Proper randomization and replication are essential to ensure the validity of results.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nANOVA can be performed using various statistical software packages, including R, SAS, SPSS, and Minitab, which provide built-in functions for conducting factorial ANOVA and generating relevant output.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nFactorial experiments and ANOVA are widely used in fields such as agriculture (to study the effects of fertilizers and irrigation), manufacturing (to optimize production processes), and psychology (to evaluate the impact of different treatments on behavior).\",300\r\n320,COMPLETE BLOCK DESIGN,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,Complete block designs are also analyzed using the analysis of variance to assess treatment differences,264\r\n582,CONFOUNDING ARRANGEMENTS,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,Analysis of Variance is a method that can be used to evaluate the effectiveness of confounding arrangements in experiments,264\r\n568,RBD,FACTORIAL EXPERIMENT,RBD is a design used in factorial experiments to manage variability among experimental units,41\r\n107,FRACTIONAL REPLICATIONS,FACTORIAL EXPERIMENT,\"**Comprehensive Summary of Fractional Replications in Factorial Experiments**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nFractional replications are a specific design approach utilized within the broader context of factorial experiments. The primary purpose of fractional replications is to reduce the number of experimental runs required while still allowing for the estimation of main effects and certain interactions among factors. This is particularly useful in situations where resources are limited or when the number of factors is large, making a full factorial design impractical.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nIn a factorial experiment, the effects of multiple factors are studied simultaneously. A full factorial design involves all possible combinations of factor levels, leading to \\(N = k^n\\) runs, where \\(k\\) is the number of levels for each factor and \\(n\\) is the number of factors. In contrast, a fractional factorial design uses only a fraction of these combinations, typically denoted as \\(N = \\frac{k^n}{p}\\), where \\(p\\) is the fraction of the full design being used. The choice of which runs to include is often determined by a defining relation that specifies which interactions are aliased with which main effects.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nFractional factorial designs are structured by selecting a subset of the full factorial combinations. The design layout is often represented in a two-level factorial format, where each factor is set at two levels (e.g., high and low). The design can be visualized using an \\(I\\)-design or \\(II\\)-design, where certain interactions are confounded with main effects or other interactions, respectively.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of fractional replications relies on several assumptions:\n1. The effects of factors are additive, meaning that interactions are either negligible or can be estimated accurately.\n2. The experimental errors are normally distributed and have constant variance (homoscedasticity).\n3. The factors are independent of each other.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nFractional replications are particularly applicable when:\n- The number of factors is large, making a full factorial design infeasible.\n- Resources (time, budget, materials) are limited.\n- The researcher is primarily interested in estimating main effects and some low-order interactions.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of fractional factorial designs typically involves:\n1. Estimating the effects of factors using regression analysis or ANOVA.\n2. Identifying significant effects and interactions, often using statistical software.\n3. Conducting diagnostics to check for model assumptions and potential confounding.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Significant reduction in the number of experimental runs required, saving time and resources.\n- Ability to explore a wide range of factors and their effects in a systematic manner.\n- Flexibility in design allows for the investigation of complex interactions with fewer resources.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- Potential for confounding, where main effects may be aliased with higher-order interactions, complicating interpretation.\n- Limited ability to estimate all interactions, particularly in higher-order designs.\n- Requires careful planning to ensure that critical interactions are not confounded.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated designs include full factorial designs, Plackett-Burman designs (for screening), and response surface methodology (RSM) for optimization. Alternatives to fractional factorial designs may include Taguchi methods or central composite designs, depending on the specific objectives of the experiment.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing fractional replications, researchers should consider:\n- The selection of factors and levels to ensure that critical interactions are not confounded.\n- The use of randomization to mitigate bias.\n- The need for replication to assess experimental error.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nMany statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and Minitab, provide tools for designing and analyzing fractional factorial experiments. These tools often include functions for generating design matrices, conducting ANOVA, and visualizing results.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nFractional replications are commonly used in industrial experiments, quality control studies, and agricultural research, where multiple factors influence outcomes, and resource constraints necessitate a more efficient experimental design. Examples include optimizing manufacturing processes, evaluating agricultural treatments, and conducting market research studies.\",40\r\n2748,DRY SOUP MIX PACKAGES,FACTORIAL EXPERIMENT,The factorial experiment was conducted to analyze the filling variability of dry soup mix packages,40\r\n110,ESTIMATES,FACTORIAL EXPERIMENT,Estimates are derived from the data collected in factorial experiments to assess the effects of factors,39\r\n2749,FACTORIAL EXPERIMENT,MIXING PORTS,Mixing ports are one of the factors tested in the factorial experiment,39\r\n2751,FACTORIAL EXPERIMENT,MIXING TIME,Mixing time is a variable considered in the factorial experiment,39\r\n2752,FACTORIAL EXPERIMENT,BATCH WEIGHT,Batch weight is a factor in the factorial experiment affecting the filling process,39\r\n2753,FACTORIAL EXPERIMENT,PACKAGE WEIGHT,Package weight is the response variable measured in the factorial experiment,39\r\n2754,FACTORIAL EXPERIMENT,FILLING VARIABILITY,Filling variability is the main focus of the factorial experiment conducted on dry soup mix packages,39\r\n2755,FACTORIAL EXPERIMENT,SIGN CHANGES,Sign changes are modifications made in the experimental design to assess the impact of different factors,39\r\n2756,FACTORIAL EXPERIMENT,DESIRABLE LAYER THICKNESS,Desirable layer thickness is a target metric evaluated in the context of the filling process,39\r\n2757,FACTORIAL EXPERIMENT,26² DESIGN,The 26² design is a specific methodology used to study the filling variability,39\r\n2758,FACTORIAL EXPERIMENT,27 IV (2)³ DESIGN,The 27 IV (2)³ design is referenced as part of the analysis of filling variability,39\r\n3459,INTERACTION EFFECT,YIELD,\"The interaction effect is related to yield, as it measures the combined influence of temperature and pressure on the yield outcome\",34\r\n68,BLOCK,REPLICATE,Replicates are used to create blocks in the factorial experiment to control for variability,30\r\n521,PLOTS,BLOCK,Each block in PBIBD consists of multiple plots where treatments are assigned.,29\r\n586,INDEPENDENT SET,BLOCK,Blocks are used to organize independent sets of treatment combinations in statistical experiments,29\r\n69,BLOCK,FACTOR AB,Factor AB is confounded in the first replicate of the experiment,28\r\n70,BLOCK,FACTOR AC,Factor AC is confounded in the second replicate of the experiment,28\r\n71,BLOCK,FACTOR BC,Factor BC is confounded in the third replicate of the experiment,28\r\n72,BLOCK,FACTOR ABC,Factor ABC is confounded in the fourth replicate of the experiment,28\r\n\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data 